1.e4 e5
  {In the first few chapters we will be looking at the King's Pawn Game, that is
  1. e4 e5. The main move played by white is 2. Nf3. But I'm not recommending that,
  and I won't recommend most mainlines as you'll see in most of this study. We go
  for the tricky lines that can take our opponent out of his opening preparation.}
  {[%csl Be4,Be5][%cal Gg1f3]}
2.Bc4
  {This opening, Bishop's Opening, is a good move for white, and better than 2. Nf3
  for two reasons: #1: It significantly reduces the theory white needs to know- as
  these are the 4 main moves played, whereas black can play several things against
  2. Nf3. #2: It will probably take black out of his theory and preparation- he was
  probably expecting the move Nf3 and won't know the proper continuations in the
  next few moves. Here black has 4 main moves. 2...Bc5 will be the main focus of
  this chapter, as well as the sideline moves. 2...Nf6 will be showcased in the NEXT
  TWO chapters, the Urusov Gambit.}
  {[%cal Gf8c5,Gg8f6,Rd7d6,Rb8c6]}
2...Bc5
  {Solid-looking move, but white has this fantastic gambit which is...}
  (2...Nc6 3.Nf3 {Now we have transposed into an Italian Game. You can research
  this opening as you please, but both sides will generally just keep developing
  and playing in the center.} {[%csl Gd4][%cal Bf8c5,Bg8f6,Gc2c3,Gd2d4,Re1g1]})
  (2...d6 3.Nf3 {[%cal Gb8d7,Gg8f6]}
  3...Nd7 4.d4 {Now we have transposed into a Philidor Defense, which will be
  covered in chapter 6.} {[%csl Rd4][%cal Rd2d4]})
3.d4!
  {d4!! Lewis gambit is very, very forcing because...black HAS to accept this. All
  other moves just lead to disaster for black, such as Bd6. Black will also be very
  badly placed if he captures with the pawn, which is being played 60% of the time!}
  {[%csl Rd4][%cal Re5d4,Bc5d4,Rc5d6,Rc5b4]}
3...Bxd4
  (3...exd4?? {This is going to be the case if your opponent sees this position
  first-time, but it's a big, big mistake. No prize for guessing white's next
  move.}
  4.Bxf7+ {BAM!}
  4...Kxf7 5.Qh5+ {[%cal Bh5f7,Bh5c5]}
  5...g6 6.Qxc5 {Black king is in the wind, and white is threatening another
  pawn!} {[%csl Gd4,Rf7][%cal Gc5d4]}
  6...Nc6 7.Nf3 {[%cal Rf3d4]}
  7...d6 8.Qd5+ {[%cal Rc8e6,Rf3g5]}
  8...Kg7
    (8...Be6 9.Ng5+ {[%csl Re6,Rf7][%cal Rg5e6,Rg5f7]})
  9.Nxd4 {[%csl Ge4,Rg7]})

  (3...Bd6?? 4.dxe5 Bxe5 5.Bxf7+ {[%csl Re8][%cal Rf7e8]}
  5...Kxf7 6.Qd5+ {[%cal Bd5f7,Bd5e5]}
  6...Kf6 7.Nf3 {[%csl Be5][%cal Bf3e5,Bd5e5]}
  7...Bd6
    (7...Qe8 8.Bg5+ {[%csl Rf6]}
    8...Kg6 9.Nxe5+ Kxg5 {[%csl Rg5]})
  8.Bg5+ {[%cal Rg5d8]}
  8...Kg6 9.Qf5+ Kh5 10.g4#)